                <pb facs="1543" n="1385" sameAs="frus:frus1906p2/pg1385"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Leishman to
                        the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1906-03-12"><hi rendition="#italic">March 12,
                        1906</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1321.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 1019, of February 21, 1906, inclosing copy of a dispatch addressed to the
                    department by Consul-General Bergholz concerning the question of the claim of
                    the American missionary established in Syria for the return of customs duties
                    that may have been improperly collected, which supplements the statement
                    inclosed in the department’s dispatch of December 4, 1905, upon this
                    subject.</p>
                <p>As the manner in which other governments treat this matter will no doubt have
                    considerable weight in assisting the department in reaching a decision as to how
                    this claim of the missionaries shall be handled, I will have to defer making a
                    report until such time as I receive definite replies to the inquiries made at
                    the several embassies.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John G. A.
                    Leishman.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
